<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">AQA-TTRL: SELF-ADAPTATION IN AUDIO QUESTION ANSWERING WITH TEST-TIME REINFORCEMENT LEARNING</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haoyu Zhang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jiaxian Guo
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yusuke Iwasawa
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yutaka Matsuo
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Audio Language Models (LALMs) demonstrate impressive general audio understanding, but once deployed, they are static and fail to improve with new real-world audio data. As traditional supervised fine-tuning is costly, we introduce a novel framework for test-time audio understanding, AQA-TTRL, where an LALM evolves on-the-fly using only unlabeled test data. It first generates pseudo-labels from the prediction via majority voting, then optimizes the model via reinforcement learning. To handle the inherent noise in these self-generated labels, we introduce a confidence-based weighting method to adjust training signals. Furthermore, a multiple-attempt sampling operation mitigates advantage collapse and stabilizes training. On the MMAU (test-mini/test), MMAR, and MMSU benchmarks, AQA-TTRL achieves significant average improvements of 4.42% for the Qwen2.5-Omni 7B model and 11.04% for the 3B model. Notably, the adapted 3B model consistently outperforms the direct inference of the unadapted 7B model, highlighting the effectiveness of previously unexplored test-time adaptations in audio understanding.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Large audio language models, reinforcement learning, test-time adaptation, self-adaptation, audio understanding

</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span>The University of Tokyo, Japan</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The application of Large Audio Language Models (LALMs) has led to notable improvements in audio understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
However, their capabilities are typically frozen after deployment; they cannot improve themselves when faced with novel, real-world test data. The traditional approach to this problem—collecting, filtering, and manually annotating new data for supervised updates—is a time-consuming and labor-intensive cycle that is fundamentally unscalable.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05478v1/overall.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:334/217;" width="334" height="217" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Role of AQA-TTRL: When faced with unseen test data, AQA-TTRL enables an automatic adaptation pipeline by eliminating the need for manual annotation.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Enabling AI systems to self-evolve on unlabeled data at test-time can significantly reduce the cost. It mirrors how humans adapt to new situations without explicit instruction.
While Test-Time Reinforcement Learning (TTRL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> has shown the potential for such self-improvement in math domains, its application to the complexities of audio remains unexplored.
In this paper, we focus on improving performance in Audio Question Answering (AQA) tasks by introducing AQA-TTRL, a novel self-adaptation method that creates a closed-loop for self-improvement. The core of AQA-TTRL is a process where the model teaches itself. It first establishes its own consensus on an answer by performing majority voting over multiple outputs, creating a pseudo-label that acts as a self-generated reward signal. This signal then guides its own policy optimization using Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. To establish a reliable self-improvement loop with the inherent noisy pseudo-labels, we further introduce two key innovations: (1) a confidence-weighting scheme to prioritize high-quality internal signals and filter out noise, and (2) a multiple-attempt sampling strategy to mitigate the “advantage collapse” problem, where advantage degenerates to zero and model updates stagnate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We provide empirical evidence that this self-improvement process is highly effective. Experimental results show that AQA-TTRL brings an average improvement of 4.42% on the Qwen2.5-Omni 7B model and 11.04% on the 3B model. The most compelling evidence of successful evolution is the adapted 3B model’s ability to surpass the performance of a static 7B model. In summary, our contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose AQA-TTRL, a novel self-adaptation method for model self-improvement in audio understanding, leveraging confidence-weighted advantage and a novel sampling strategy to create a stable and effective test-time learning loop.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide the first empirical evidence that LALMs can achieve significant self-improvement in complex AQA tasks, demonstrating the viability of this new paradigm on the MMAU (test-mini/test) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, MMAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and MMSU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> benchmarks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We justify our approach by demonstrating a strong positive correlation between model confidence and correctness on test data, validating the core mechanism that drives our self-improvement framework.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Large Audio Language Models</span>.
Large Audio Language Models (LALMs) demonstrate strong abilities in understanding and reasoning across a variety of tasks involving auditory input such as speech, sound, and music.
They typically use LLMs as their backbone for understanding and text generation, while incorporating an audio encoder to provide auditory perception ability. Representative models include LTU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, SALMONN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, Audio Flamingo series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, DeSTA series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, Qwen Audio series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and Qwen2.5-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
By formulating diverse tasks as text generation, LALMs overcome task-specific constraints.
These works improve the accuracy on traditional audio tasks and are capable of processing open-ended Audio Question Answering (AQA).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards</span>.
Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has been proven effective in improving the ability of LLMs.
Deepseek Math <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and Deepseek R1-Zeros <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> use RLVR to improve the model’s reasoning ability without reward models.
SEED-GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> applies semantic entropy to represent uncertainty for calculating the advantage.
TTRL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> uses RLVR with majority voting at test time to boost the performance in math benchmarks.
Spurious Rewards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> shows that even weak or spurious labels can improve model performance with RLVR.
For the combination of LALMs and RLVR, R1-AQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> shows that GRPO can have better performance than supervised finetuning on MMAU benchmarks.
Omni-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> gains the SOTA on MMAU and MMAR by applying GRPO with an LLM augmented dataset.
In contrast to prior work that relies on labeled training data for RL post-training, our method is under a label-free training paradigm.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>METHOD</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first present the problem setting of adapting the model at test time using unlabeled test data.
Next, we present the overview of our method, covering pseudo-label generation and model update, followed by its two key innovations: Confidence-Weighted Advantage and Multiple-Attempt Sampling.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Setting</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Audio understanding models are improved by collecting target-domain audios and questions, annotating them, and updating the model with labeled pairs.
However, this procedure is costly and limits robustness.
If the model can adapt itself at test time <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">with unlabeled test data only</span>, it not only reduces annotation costs but also improves robustness to unforeseen domain shifts.
In this work, we focus on Audio Question Answering (AQA) as a representative task, and our problem setting is to improve AQA performance through test-time adaptation under a label-free paradigm.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Overview of the AQA-TTRL</h3>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05478v1/ttrl2.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:334/185;" width="334" height="185" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the AQA-TTRL. The framework derives pseudo-labels with confidence through majority voting, conducts multiple-attempt sampling for effective response generation, and adjusts the learning signal by weighting it with confidence.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to improve the model quality without annotated data, AQA-TTRL contains two stages as Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Overview of the AQA-TTRL ‣ 3 METHOD ‣ AQA-TTRL: SELF-ADAPTATION IN AUDIO QUESTION ANSWERING WITH TEST-TIME REINFORCEMENT LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows: 1) Pseudo-labels generation. 2) Pseudo-labels Guided Model Updates. Motivated by prior work <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Self-Consistency</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, we first generate the multiple model’s prediction <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for a given input, and then conduct majority voting on these responses to establish a consensus answer, which serves as the pseudo-label from the model’s own consensus:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{y}=\arg\max_{c\in\mathcal{Y}}\textstyle\sum_{m=1}^{M}\mathbf{1}\{y_{m}=c\},\quad\hat{y}:\text{pseudo-label}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mtext>pseudo-label</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\arg\max_{c\in\mathcal{Y}}\textstyle\sum_{m=1}^{M}\mathbf{1}\{y_{m}=c\},\quad\hat{y}:\text{pseudo-label}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For model updates, we adopt Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
Unlike supervised fine-tuning, which directly imitates noisy pseudo labels, GRPO optimizes reward signals and thus provides a more noise-robust foundation for test-time adaptation.
For each audio-question pair, the policy model <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> generates a set of responses <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o_{1},o_{2},\ldots,o_{G}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{1},o_{2},\ldots,o_{G}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, each referred to as a rollout. Subsequently, the reward score <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated by comparing the response <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with the pre-generated pseudo-label <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> using the reward function. Following the same setup as previous work<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, a format reward and an accuracy reward are used.</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=r_{acc}(o_{i},\hat{y})+r_{format}(o_{i},\hat{y})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=r_{acc}(o_{i},\hat{y})+r_{format}(o_{i},\hat{y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The advantage <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> is derived by normalizing the rewards <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{G}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{G}\}</annotation></semantics></math> within each group. The policy model <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is then optimized by maximizing the following objective:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>GRPO</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\left[q,\{o_{i}\}\right]}\left\{\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{\left|o_{i}\right|}\sum_{t=1}^{\left|o_{i}\right|}(\min\left[c_{i,t}A_{i,t},\right.\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\left[q,\{o_{i}\}\right]}\left\{\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{\left|o_{i}\right|}\sum_{t=1}^{\left|o_{i}\right|}(\min\left[c_{i,t}A_{i,t},\right.\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left.\mathrm{clip}\left(c_{i,t},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\right)A_{i,t}\right]-\beta\,\mathbb{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(\pi_{\theta}\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\right))\Bigg\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left.\mathrm{clip}\left(c_{i,t},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\right)A_{i,t}\right]-\beta\,\mathbb{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(\pi_{\theta}\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\right))\Bigg\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{where}\;c_{i,t}=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i}|q)}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(o_{i}|q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>where</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{where}\;c_{i,t}=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i}|q)}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(o_{i}|q)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are two hyperparameters to prevent the model update of any iteration from deviating too far from the base model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, a naive implementation of pseudo-label guided learning faces two critical challenges. First, the self-generated pseudo-labels are inherently noisy, and learning from them directly is not desired. Second, the learning signal can vanish if the model’s policy converges on a single answer within a limited sampling group, <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, advantage collapse.
To solve these, we introduce two corresponding innovations to enable training that is both noise-robust and effective:
(1) Confidence-Weighted Advantage (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Confidence-Weighted Advantage ‣ 3 METHOD ‣ AQA-TTRL: SELF-ADAPTATION IN AUDIO QUESTION ANSWERING WITH TEST-TIME REINFORCEMENT LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>), which approximates label quality through estimated confidence scores and adjusts the training signal accordingly, thereby enhancing robustness to label noise;
and (2) Multiple-Attempt Sampling (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Multiple-Attempt Sampling ‣ 3 METHOD ‣ AQA-TTRL: SELF-ADAPTATION IN AUDIO QUESTION ANSWERING WITH TEST-TIME REINFORCEMENT LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>), which prevents training signal vanishing under uniform rewards within a group by generating multiple response groups, thereby mitigating the risk of uniform rewards.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Confidence-Weighted Advantage</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the confidence-weighted advantage, we use the estimated confidence of the pseudo-label to evaluate the quality. Since LALMs output the answers directly in text form, the confidence scores cannot be obtained explicitly. Instead, we estimate it via the consistency with the majority-voting results:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Conf=\frac{\sum_{m=1}^{M}\mathbf{1}(y_{m}=\hat{y})}{M}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Conf=\frac{\sum_{m=1}^{M}\mathbf{1}(y_{m}=\hat{y})}{M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, to incorporate label reliability into model update, we formulate the Confidence-Weighted Advantage as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i,t}=\frac{r_{i}-mean(\{r_{1},r_{2},...,r_{G}\})}{std(\{r_{1},r_{2},...,r_{G}\})}\cdot f(Conf)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i,t}=\frac{r_{i}-mean(\{r_{1},r_{2},...,r_{G}\})}{std(\{r_{1},r_{2},...,r_{G}\})}\cdot f(Conf)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Given the uncertain relationship between confidence and label quality, we explore multiple candidate functions <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>—exponential, square root and linear—for robustness across benchmarks. The comparative performance is demonstrated in the ablation experiment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We build on the observation that higher prediction confidence is generally associated with higher accuracy.
As we adopt the majority-voted answers as the training labels, labels with higher confidence are more likely to align with the ground truth label.
When the model answers a question with low confidence, the label is still valuable, but the model update should be more conservative.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Multiple-Attempt Sampling</h3>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Multiple-Attempts Sampling</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">input:</span> policy <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, old policy <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, group size <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, dataset <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\{(q_{n},Aud_{n},\hat{y}_{n},Conf_{n})\}_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{(q_{n},Aud_{n},\hat{y}_{n},Conf_{n})\}_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{n},Aud_{n},\hat{y}_{n},Conf_{n})\in D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{n},Aud_{n},\hat{y}_{n},Conf_{n})\in D</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o^{(1)}_{1},\ldots,o^{(1)}_{G}\}\sim\pi_{\theta}(o\mid q_{n},Aud_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o^{(1)}_{1},\ldots,o^{(1)}_{G}\}\sim\pi_{\theta}(o\mid q_{n},Aud_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>; <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> group1
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o^{(2)}_{1},\ldots,o^{(2)}_{G}\}\sim\pi_{\theta}(o\mid q_{n},Aud_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o^{(2)}_{1},\ldots,o^{(2)}_{G}\}\sim\pi_{\theta}(o\mid q_{n},Aud_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>; <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> group2
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o^{(3)}_{1},\ldots,o^{(3)}_{G}\}\sim\pi_{\theta}(o\mid q_{n},Aud_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o^{(3)}_{1},\ldots,o^{(3)}_{G}\}\sim\pi_{\theta}(o\mid q_{n},Aud_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>; <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> group3
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> all_same(group1) <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> all_same(group2) <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
                 <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\leftarrow\{o^{(3)}_{1},\ldots,o^{(3)}_{G}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\leftarrow\{o^{(3)}_{1},\ldots,o^{(3)}_{G}\}</annotation></semantics></math>;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
