</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When a limited number of responses are sampled during training, high-confidence questions often collapse to identical outputs.
In this situation, advantage collapse arises: the advantage degenerates to zero by definition, leading the model to stop learning from current data.
Since high-confidence samples are generally of higher quality, leveraging them effectively is crucial for successful self-adaptation.
To relieve this problem, we propose a multiple-attempt sampling operation.
As shown in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.4 Multiple-Attempt Sampling ‣ 3 METHOD ‣ AQA-TTRL: SELF-ADAPTATION IN AUDIO QUESTION ANSWERING WITH TEST-TIME REINFORCEMENT LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we first generate three groups with equal sample size. If all samples of the first group are identical, we fall back to the second.
If the second is also identical, we then use the third.
Although this process cannot guarantee non-identical samples within a group, it can relieve the issue by offering two fallback attempts.
Moreover, when combined with confidence weighting, the process becomes more effective, as it efficiently preserves the amplified training signal from high-quality samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>EXPERIMENTS</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce our experiment setting and the performance on the following AQA benchmarks: MMAU-v05.15.25 (test-mini/test), MMAR, and MMSU.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experiment Setting</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted the training with 4 H100 GPUs. We use Qwen2.5-Omni 7B and Qwen2.5-Omni 3B<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> as the base model for adaptation training. The batch size is 1 per GPU and the accumulation step is 2. The sampling temperature is set to 1.0 for both majority voting and rollout (response generation). The pseudo-label is obtained through majority voting over 64 generated predictions in advance, then the rollout for model update is set to 4 generations. We train for 500 steps on all benchmarks. We fix the reporting step according to dataset scale: the 100th iteration for smaller datasets (MMAU test-mini/MMAR) to avoid overfitting, and the final iteration for larger ones (MMAU test/MMSU) to ensure sufficient training. Following the setting of previous research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is 0.2 and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is 0. The prompt is: “{<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">question</span>}<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Please choose the answer from the following options: </span>{<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">choice string</span>}<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Output the final answer in <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>answer<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>/answer<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>”. Similar to the previous research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, we disable thinking and let the model output the answer directly.</p>
</div>
</section>
