<td id="S4.T1.5.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.5pt;padding-right:4.5pt;"><span id="S4.T1.5.9.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">64.86</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We take Direct Inference (DI) of the unadapted model as the baseline and use reproduced results for a consistent inference setting.
As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Main results across different benchmarks ‣ 4 EXPERIMENTS ‣ AQA-TTRL: SELF-ADAPTATION IN AUDIO QUESTION ANSWERING WITH TEST-TIME REINFORCEMENT LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, for the 7B model, our method demonstrates its effectiveness by outperforming DI across all benchmarks with an average improvement of 4.42%.
For the 3B model, the improvement is more pronounced, reaching an average improvement of 11.04% across all benchmarks.
Interestingly, we observe that the self-adapted 3B model can even surpass the performance of the direct inference of the 7B unadapted model.
Our method is effective because GRPO assigns negative rewards when predictions diverge from pseudo labels, yielding reliable supervision even without ground-truth annotations.
These results demonstrate the potential of LALMs to self-improve under a label-free training paradigm at test time, and further suggest that small models can also achieve comparative performance without requiring manual annotation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We can also observe that our method significantly outperforms Direct Inference with Majority Voting (DIMV), which serves as the training label.
This highlights the advantage of adopting reinforcement learning: if the model were updated via supervised fine-tuning, the upper bound of performance would be determined by the training labels.
However, reinforcement learning enables the model to go beyond the limitations of the noisy training labels.
We attribute this improvement to the ability of reinforcement learning to tolerate a certain degree of label inaccuracy and to capture the underlying distribution rather than merely imitating.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
