<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Weight function <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> comparison.</span> We compare the performance of three functions <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>: square root, linear and exponential. Although all three functions are monotonically increasing, they differ in curvature and boundary points. Shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2.st1" title="In Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 4 EXPERIMENTS ‣ AQA-TTRL: SELF-ADAPTATION IN AUDIO QUESTION ANSWERING WITH TEST-TIME REINFORCEMENT LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a>, exponential achieves the highest accuracy across all benchmarks. Compared with the square root, linear indicates a general advantage, but performs worse in MMAU test-mini and MMAR. These results indicate that exponential weighting provides a proper training signal by sufficiently utilizing low-confidence labels while simultaneously amplifying the contribution of high-confidence labels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of Confidence-Weighted Advantage and Multiple-Attempt Sampling.</span>
As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2.st2" title="In Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 4 EXPERIMENTS ‣ AQA-TTRL: SELF-ADAPTATION IN AUDIO QUESTION ANSWERING WITH TEST-TIME REINFORCEMENT LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a>, we explore the contribution of Confidence-Weighted Advantage and Multiple-Attempt Sampling in this section.
Vanilla GRPO with Majority Voting (G-MV) has the lowest average accuracy.
Adding Multiple-Attempt Sampling improves performance on most benchmarks, except for MMAR.
Meanwhile, adding confidence weight improves the performance in MMAU test-mini and MMAR, but does not lead to gains on the benchmarks with larger amounts of data.
A possible explanation is that confidence weighting enlarges the gap between different samples, which worsens the training dynamics when advantage collapse occurs.
The combination of these two modules consistently improves performance with an average gain of 1.31%.
This ablation study reveals that confidence-weighted advantage effectively adjusts the training signal and multiple-attempt sampling enhances training effectiveness, yielding the combined benefits of both modules.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Correlation between Pseudo-Label Quality and Confidence</h3>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05478v1/org2_reg.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:334/127;" width="334" height="127"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Accuracy vs. Confidence of Pseudo-Labels</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05478v1/mmsu.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:334/118;" width="334" height="118"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Case study: accuracy over training steps (MMSU).
To preserve the label-free setting, we report performance at the fixed 500th step.
However, better performance may occur at intermediate steps.
Given that MMSU contains 5,000 samples and the global training batch size is 8, this suggests that the model can reach strong performance before fully traversing the dataset.
This highlights an avenue for future work—designing label-free model selection strategies that enhance training effectiveness while reducing computational cost.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To justify the effectiveness of the confidence weighting mechanism, we investigate the relationship between confidence and accuracy of the pseudo-label.
Confidence score is grouped by 5% intervals, with the mean accuracy calculated within each interval.
Each interval yields a confidence–accuracy pair defined by its midpoint and mean accuracy, on which a regression line is fitted for correlation analysis.
Because MMAU test is evaluated by an online system, where ground truth labels are unavailable, we only analyze the remaining test datasets.
Shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.4 Correlation between Pseudo-Label Quality and Confidence ‣ 4 EXPERIMENTS ‣ AQA-TTRL: SELF-ADAPTATION IN AUDIO QUESTION ANSWERING WITH TEST-TIME REINFORCEMENT LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we find that there is a strong positive correlation between confidence and accuracy across different test datasets.
This is consistent with our hypothesis that answers generated with higher confidence tend to exhibit higher accuracy.
As training signals from pseudo-labels with higher confidence are closer to the supervised optimization, such signals should be encouraged to contribute more to the model optimization.
It confirms that the confidence weighting mechanism with reinforcement learning can effectively emphasize informative samples and tolerate noisy samples, aligning the model update with the inherent data distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>CONCLUSION</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose AQA-TTRL, a self-adaptation framework that enables model to learn and evolve on-the-fly using only the unlabeled data on audio question answering tasks (AQA). Our framework establishes a closed-loop learning process by generating confidence-weighted pseudo-labels from majority voting to guide policy optimization with GRPO. We further introduce two methods to increase the reliability of the loop: 1) a confidence-weighting scheme to handle noisy self-generated signals and 2) a multiple-attempt sampling strategy to mitigate the advantage collapse problem inherent in this setting. We demonstrated the effectiveness of AQA-TTRL on the MMAU (test-mini/test), MMAR, and MMSU benchmarks, achieving significant average improvements of 4.42% on the Qwen2.5-Omni 7B model and 11.04% on the 3B model. Most notably, the self-improved 3B model surpassed the direct inference performance of the larger, unadapted 7B model, proving that targeted, on-the-fly evolution can be a more efficient path to robust performance than model scaling alone.
Despite these promising results, our method adopts a fixed step selection to preserve the label-free paradigm.
As future work, developing flexible, label-free model selection methods has the potential to yield performance gains and lower training costs.</p>
</div>
</section>
